.TH PI-STACK 1 "July 2026" "pi-stack" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pi-stack \- run the pi coding agent in a Docker sandbox and manage its host services
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pi-stack
.RI [ \-\-profile\ NAME ]
.br
.B pi-stack
.RI [ \-\-profile\ NAME ]
.B run
.RI [ DIR ]
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ \-\- " pi-args..." ]
.br
.B pi-stack
.RI [ \-\-profile\ NAME ]
.I DIR
.br
.B pi-stack
.RI [ \-\-profile\ NAME ]
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pi-stack
is the launcher for the
.B pi
coding agent in a Docker Sandbox managed by
.BR sbx (1).
It also controls the host-side memory service on port 11435 and knowledge
service on port 11436.
.PP
With no command,
.B pi-stack
shows status.  It never launches a sandbox.  Launching is explicit through
.B run
or through the bare existing-directory form.  On a host without a configuration
file, the no-argument form points you at
.B pi-stack run
(which offers conversational onboarding on the first run).
A bare non-directory word is an error, not a workspace name.
.SH PREREQUISITES
Two things are required before an agent can run:
.TP
.BR sbx (1)
The Docker Sandboxes CLI must be installed and on
.BR PATH .
It creates and runs the sandbox and injects provider keys through its network
proxy.
.TP
At least one provider key
The agent needs at least one model provider.  Keys are stored on the host as
.B sbx
secrets and injected into every sandbox by the proxy; they never appear in a
configuration file or inside the sandbox.  Set one with, for example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sbx secret set -g anthropic -t "sk-..."
.RE
.fi
.SH QUICK START
The shortest path from nothing to a working agent:
.PP
.nf
.RS
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mcavage/pi-stack/main/install.sh | sh
sbx secret set -g anthropic -t "sk-..."
pi-stack run
.RE
.fi
.PP
Set at least one provider key, then
.B run
launches the sandbox and drops you into the agent.  On the first run the agent
offers to onboard you (opt-in); decline and you get a normal session.
.SH ONBOARDING
Onboarding is OPT-IN and happens IN-SESSION: the agent offers it on the first
.B pi-stack run
(say "onboard me" any time to redo it).  It seeds your identity and preferences
into the memory service (so future sessions recall you) and may propose
host-config changes by writing
.B .pi-stack/onboarding.json
in the workspace, which the host validates against an allowlist and applies
under a confirmation gate on the next run.
.PP
.B run
never forces onboarding.  Provider keys,
.BR serve ,
and the memory service are host-side prerequisites.  For automation and CI,
.B pi-stack onboard
applies deterministic host config from flags without a sandbox or a prompt (the
former
.B pi-stack setup
is a deprecated alias for it; the interactive wizard was removed).
.SH CREDENTIAL MODEL
There are two distinct kinds of secret, and they live in different places.
.PP
Provider keys (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, GitHub) are
.B sbx
secrets stored on the host and injected into every sandbox by the network proxy.
They never appear in a configuration file or inside the sandbox.  Manage them
with
.BR sbx ,
not with
.BR pi-stack .
.PP
Integration credentials (for host MCP servers such as Slack) are 1Password
references kept in
.BR op-refs.env .
The file stores only
.B op://vault/item/field
references, never the secret value; the value is read from 1Password by
.BR op (1)
at the moment the integration starts and passed in as an environment variable,
so it never touches disk.  Some integrations, such as Google Workspace on
macOS, can use the system keychain instead and need no refs entry.  Manage the
refs template with
.BR "pi-stack secret" .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "status, st"
.TP
.BR "pi-stack status" " [" \-\-json "]"
Show a read-only snapshot of services, provider keys, knowledge bundles, MCP
registration, the active profile, and running
.B pi-stack-*
sandboxes.  The
.B \-\-json
option emits the snapshot as JSON.  This is also the no-command action.
.SS ls
.TP
.BR "pi-stack ls" " [" \-\-json "]"
List the
.B pi-stack-*
sandboxes on this host (name, state, workspace dir).  For every sbx sandbox, use
.BR "sbx ls" .
.SS rm
.TP
.BR "pi-stack rm" " " name... " [" \-\-all "] [" \-\-except " " name "]"
Remove pi-stack sandboxes (via
.BR "sbx rm \-f" ).
Scoped to
.B pi-stack-*
names.
.B \-\-all
removes every pi-stack sandbox;
.B \-\-except
keeps one (e.g. the box you are in).
.SS onboard
.TP
.BR "pi-stack onboard" " [" \-\-account " " email "] [" \-\-knowledge " " path-or-url "] [" \-\-mcp " " name "] [" \-\-model " " ollama-model "] [" \-\-apply "] [" \-\-yes "]"
Host-side onboarding: the deterministic HOST configuration and the flag-driven
path for automation and CI.  The CONVERSATIONAL onboarding is in-session — run
.B pi-stack run
(it offers onboarding on the first run) or say "onboard me" to the agent.
.B \-\-account
sets the Google Workspace account and enables gog.
.B \-\-knowledge
scaffolds or selects the global OKF bundle.
.B \-\-mcp
enables an allowlisted MCP server (repeatable).
.B \-\-model
sets the ollama-bridge model.
.B \-\-apply
applies a pending
.B .pi-stack/onboarding.json
in the current directory (the control-plane proposal the in-sandbox onboarding
agent writes), under a confirmation gate.
.B \-\-yes
(alias
.BR \-\-non-interactive )
never prompts.
The former
.B pi-stack setup
is a deprecated alias for this command; the interactive wizard is gone.
.SS doctor
.TP
.BR "pi-stack doctor" " [" \-\-json "]"
Check provider keys, Ollama and its configured models, memory, Google Workspace,
1Password refs, and MCP registrations.  Print repair commands for failed checks.
.B \-\-json
emits a machine-readable report.
.SS run
.TP
.BR "pi-stack run" " [" DIR "] [" \-\-dev "] [" \-\-skills " " DIR "]... [" \-\-kit " " K "]... [" \-\-mcp " " M "]... [" \-\-name " " N "] [" \-\-model " " M "] [" \-\-replace "] [" \-\- " " pi-args... "]"
Launch a sandbox for
.IR DIR ,
which defaults to the current directory.  An explicit directory must exist.
Arguments after
.B \-\-
are passed to
.BR pi .
.PP
Lifecycle matches sbx's own re-attach model.  When no sandbox of the resolved
name exists, it is created and the flags below apply.  When one already
exists — running
.B or
stopped —
.B run
RE-ATTACHES to it as-is instead of refusing or recreating: sbx reads the agent
from the existing sandbox's own spec, so
.BR \-\-kit ,
.BR \-\-mcp ,
.BR \-\-template ,
and the create-only skill flags are not re-sent.  Use
.B \-\-replace
to force a recreate instead.
.TP
.B \-\-dev
Use the local checkout kit and load its skills live.  A checkout must be found
through
.BR PI_STACK_DEV_ROOT ,
the current directory or an ancestor, or the launcher's installed location.
Applies only to a fresh create; use
.B \-\-replace
to pick it up on an existing sandbox.
.TP
.BI \-\-skills " DIR"
Mount and load an additional skill tree.  Repeatable.  Create-only; see
.BR \-\-replace .
.TP
.BI \-\-kit " K"
Replace automatic kit selection with a path or git URL.  Repeatable.
Create-only; see
.BR \-\-replace .
.TP
.BI \-\-mcp " M"
Attach an MCP server when creating the sandbox.  Repeatable; attachment is used
only when
.B SBX_MCP_URL
is set.  Create-only; see
.BR \-\-replace .
.TP
.BI \-\-name " N"
Use
.I N
as the sandbox name.  Otherwise the launcher derives
.BI pi-stack- workspace
and appends a non-default profile name.
.TP
.BI \-\-model " M"
Pass
.I M
to
.B pi
as its active model.  Unlike the other flags above, this is
.B not
create-only: it is a pi runtime argument, so it still reaches
.B pi
on a re-attach too.
.TP
.B \-\-replace
Recreate the sandbox (
.B sbx rm \-f
then create) instead of re-attaching to an existing one, whether it is running
or stopped.  This is the way to pick up a changed
.BR \-\-kit / \-\-mcp /
create-only flag.
.SS host
.TP
.BR "pi-stack host" " [" DIR "] [" \-\-model " " M "] [" \-\- " " pi-args... "]"
EXPERT, DANGEROUS.  Run
.B pi
directly on this machine \(em no sandbox, no network fence, real credentials.
A narrow escape hatch for developing pi-stack itself (which needs the host's
Docker, sbx, and make); never a fallback when
.B sbx
is missing.  Gated off by default: enable deliberately with
.BR "pi-stack config set host.enabled true" .
The launch refuses a workspace at
.BR $HOME ,
.BR / ,
or
.BR /etc ,
or one inside or containing SSH/cloud/password-store secret directories
(symlinks are resolved before the check).  Subagent spawning is disabled, a
host-guard extension is always loaded, and a host-specific system preamble
replaces the sandbox's disposable-VM context.  These are guardrails against
accidents,
.B not
a security boundary \(em for anything you wouldn't hand a shell to, use
.BR "pi-stack run" .
Cloud keys come from op:// refs in
.B hostmode.env
next to config.toml, resolved just-in-time by
.BR "op run" ;
without that file the session is Ollama-only (valid).
.TP
.B "pi-stack host setup"
Provision the host agent directory at
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/pi-stack/host-agent
(default
.BR ~/.local/state/pi-stack/host-agent ):
symlinks the checkout's harness dirs (skills, agents, extensions, themes), a
host-specific
.BR settings.json ,
a
.B sessions/
directory, and the curated pi extension packages (printing the exact install
commands when
.B pi
is unavailable).
.TP
Naming: this verb
.RB ( "pi-stack host" ,
the agent running ON the host) is distinct from
.B pi-stack-host
(the compiled Go daemon binary) and from
.B "pi-stack serve"
(which runs the host services memory/knowledge).  Three names, three things.
.SS serve
.TP
.BR "pi-stack serve" " [" args... "]"
Execute
.B pi-stack-host serve
with unchanged arguments.  With no service arguments, the host configuration
selects services.  Memory is enabled by default; knowledge runs when enabled.
.TP
.B "pi-stack serve stop"
Read the supervisor pidfile and verify that the process is
.BR "pi-stack-host serve"
before sending SIGTERM.  If neither /proc nor ps can identify the process, it
REFUSES to signal (remove the pidfile to override) rather than trust the pidfile
alone.  It sends SIGKILL only if the process does not exit after about five
seconds and a repeated identity check still confirms the process is ours.
Extra arguments are rejected.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack serve status" " [" \-\-json "]"
Report the supervisor PID and memory and knowledge port state.
.B \-\-json
emits JSON.
.TP
.B "pi-stack serve install"
Install the services as a MANAGED login service: a launchd LaunchAgent
.RB ( com.pi-stack.serve )
on macOS, a systemd \-\-user unit
.RB ( pi-stack-serve.service )
on Linux.  Starts at login, auto-restarts, and starts immediately.  Logs go to
.I ~/.local/state/pi-stack/serve.log
on both macOS and Linux \(em the SAME file the lazy auto-start uses, so there
is one place to look regardless of how serve was started (on Linux,
.RI ( "journalctl \-\-user \-u pi-stack-serve" )
also works as a secondary view).  Idempotent; re-running reinstalls with the current binary path.
Before installing, a lazily-started background daemon is stopped first and a
FOREGROUND
.B pi-stack serve
is refused (stop it and re-run) \(em installing over a live daemon would make
the auto-restarting unit crash-loop on its ports.  After installing, the
command verifies (bounded) that the services actually came up and reports
honestly when they did not.  The generated unit captures the install-time
environment the daemon needs to read the SAME config and ports as the
launcher: an absolute
.B PI_STACK_CONFIG
is always rendered, plus
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ,
.BR MEMORY_DB ,
.BR MEMORY_PORT ,
.BR KNOWLEDGE_PORT ,
and
.B OLLAMA_HOST
when set at install time.
.TP
.B "pi-stack serve uninstall"
Remove the managed login service (bootout/disable and delete the unit file).
.PP
LAZY AUTO-START: when the managed service is not installed,
.BR "pi-stack run" ,
.BR "pi-stack memory" ,
and
.B "pi-stack knowledge query"
auto-start a detached
.B pi-stack-host serve
if the configured service ports are down (the ollama-CLI model), waiting
briefly for health and printing progress.  Its log is
.IR ~/.local/state/pi-stack/serve.log .
Opt out with the
.B PI_STACK_NO_AUTOSERVE
environment variable or
.BR "pi-stack config set host.autoserve false" .
After a daemon-affecting config write
.RB ( services ,
.BR memory_watcher_model ,
.BR memory_embed_model ,
.BR knowledge_bundles )
the launcher restarts a managed or lazily-started daemon automatically so the
change takes effect; a foreground serve is never killed \(em you are told to
restart it.
.B "pi-stack knowledge init"
and
.B "pi-stack knowledge use"
save
.B knowledge_bundles
too and propagate the same way.
.SS memory
The aliases
.BR mem ,
.BR search ,
.BR add ,
.BR rm ,
.BR promotable ,
and
.B status
map respectively to
.BR memory ,
.BR recall ,
.BR remember ,
.BR forget ,
.BR learnings ,
and
.BR stats .
.TP
.BR "pi-stack memory recall" " " query " [" \-\-limit " " N "] [" \-\-project " " P "] [" \-\-json "]"
Search stored facts.  The default limit is 8.  The default project filter is
empty.  Short aliases
.B \-n
and
.B \-p
are accepted for
.B \-\-limit
and
.BR \-\-project .
.TP
.BR "pi-stack memory remember" " " text... " [" \-\-json "]"
Store a fact with source
.B cli
in the active profile.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack memory forget" " " id " [" \-\-json "]"
Delete the fact matching an ID or ID prefix in the active profile.  The
.B \-\-json
option emits the raw result.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack memory learnings" " [" \-\-min " " N "] [" \-\-json "]"
List recurring, promotable learnings.  The default minimum frequency is 3.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack memory stats" " [" \-\-json "]"
Print counts by kind and durability for the active profile.
.PP
Memory RPC commands return status 3 when the memory daemon cannot be reached.
Full backup and restore are now the top-level
.B backup
and
.B restore
verbs, not memory subcommands.
.SS backup
.TP
.BR "pi-stack backup" " [" \-\-out " " PATH "] [" \-\-keep " " N "]"
Create a hot, verified FULL backup, safe while
.B serve
holds the database.  It packages a SQLite
.B "VACUUM INTO"
snapshot of the memory database, the
.B config.toml
(profiles), the
.B op-refs.env
(references only), and a
.B manifest.json
(format version 2, recording the profile names and a note of each configured
knowledge bundle's path and git remote) into a gzip tar archive.  Knowledge
bundle contents are NOT archived: a bundle is a git repository and git is its
backup; the manifest records only where it lives.  The default output is
.BR ~/.local/share/pi-stack/backups/pi-stack-backup- timestamp .tar.gz;
the default retention count is 7.  The alias
.B \-o
is accepted for
.BR \-\-out .
The command execes the sibling
.B pi-stack-host
binary and propagates its status.
.SS restore
.TP
.BR "pi-stack restore" " " archive " [" \-\-force "]"
Restore a FULL backup by safe swap.  The command refuses to run while the memory
service is listening and refuses to replace an existing database without
.BR \-\-force .
With
.B \-\-force
(the alias
.B \-f
is accepted), it moves the current database to a timestamped backup first.  The
.B config.toml
and
.B op-refs.env
are always moved aside to a timestamped
.B .bak
file before the archived versions are written, so profiles come back and the
change is reversible.  A format-version-1 (memory-only manifest) archive still
restores.  The command reports the restored memory rows, the config profiles now
present, and the recorded knowledge-bundle locations, then execes the sibling
.B pi-stack-host
binary and propagates its status.
.SS knowledge
The alias
.B kb
maps to
.BR knowledge ;
.B search
maps to
.BR query ;
and
.B push
maps to
.BR sync .
.TP
.BR "pi-stack knowledge init" " [" DIR "]"
Scaffold an OKF bundle, initialize Git when available, add it to
.BR knowledge_bundles ,
and enable the knowledge service.  The default directory is
.B knowledge
under the configuration directory.  Existing bundle files are not overwritten.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack knowledge use" " " path-or-git-url
Set a global bundle reference.  A local path is made absolute but is not checked
for existence or OKF contents.  A Git URL is cloned or updated below the
configuration directory in
.BR knowledge-cache .
.TP
.BR "pi-stack knowledge use" " " \-\-project " " path-or-git-url " [" \-\-dir " " D "]"
Write
.B .pi-stack/knowledge
under
.I D
(default current directory) without changing global configuration.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack knowledge ls" " [" \-\-json "]"
List the active profile's configured bundles, service state, and any project
pointer.  JSON output is selected with
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.BR "pi-stack knowledge query" " " text... " [" \-\-limit " " N "] [" \-\-json "]"
Search the daemon within the active profile's bundles.  The default limit is 5;
.B \-n
is an alias for
.BR \-\-limit .
An unreachable daemon returns status 3.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack knowledge sync" " [" \-m " " MSG "] [" \-\-bundle " " D "] [" \-\-allow-main "]"
Commit and push a bundle.  Without
.BR \-\-bundle ,
exactly one bundle must be configured for the active profile.  The default
commit message contains a UTC timestamp.  By default, create and push a
.B knowledge/sync-*
review branch and print a
.B gh pr create
hint.
.B \-\-allow-main
instead commits and pushes the current branch; detached HEAD is refused.
.B \-m
is an alias for
.BR \-\-message .
.TP
.BR "pi-stack knowledge remote" " [" \-\-bundle " " D "]"
Show the selected bundle's origin URL.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack knowledge remote set" " " url " [" \-\-bundle " " D "]"
Add or replace the selected bundle's origin URL.
.SS agent
Manage subagents as first-class objects.  An agent stores an
.B intent
(a complexity tier), not a pinned model; the router derives its default model
from a hand-maintained scorecard.  Agents live in
.B ./agents
(or
.BR $PI_STACK_AGENTS_DIR ).
.TP
.BR "pi-stack agent ls" " [" \-\-json "]"
List the roster with each agent's resolved model and WHY (its intent, and
whether the resolver fell back).
.TP
.BR "pi-stack agent new" " " NAME " [" \-\-intent " " I "] [" \-\-description " " D "] [" \-\-tools " " a,b "] [" \-\-budget " " USD "] [" \-\-interactive "]"
Scaffold an agent markdown file.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack agent edit" " " NAME " [" \-\-intent " " I "] [" \-\-description " " D "] [" \-\-tools " " a,b "] [" \-\-budget " " USD "] [" \-\-model " " M "]"
Change fields without hand-editing frontmatter.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack agent rm" " " NAME " " \-\-yes
Remove an agent.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack agent reassess" " [" \-\-model " " NEW "]"
Re-resolve the roster under the current policy/scorecard (zero spend) and
recompile.
.BR \-\-model
points you at hand-editing the scorecard instead of measuring anything.  Prints
the routing diff.
.SS route
The model router: turn a declared
.B intent
(a hard cost/latency/accuracy constraint) into a concrete model, from a registry
of callable models and their prices plus a measured scorecard.  Replaces
hand-pinning a model on every agent.  Execes the sibling
.B pi-stack-host
binary.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack route pick" " " intent " [" \-\-json "]"
Resolve one intent to a model and print the rationale + ranked alternatives.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack route compile" " [" \-\-out " " PATH "]"
Resolve every intent and write the intent-to-model map
.RB ( routing.json ).
Use
.B "\-\-out ./routing.json"
to update the baked file, then
.B "make load"
to bake it into the image.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack route show" " [" \-\-json "]"
Print the registry, scorecard, and resolved-intent table.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack route models" " [" \-\-json "]"
List the model registry.
.SS mcp
.TP
.BR "pi-stack mcp register" " [" name... "]"
Register named local stdio MCP servers with the sbx gateway.  With no names,
use the active profile's configured MCP list.  Remote gateway-catalog names are
skipped.  Registration requires
.BR SBX_MCP_URL .
.TP
.B "pi-stack mcp ls"
Execute
.BR "sbx mcp ls" .
If
.B sbx
is unavailable, print the command to run on the host.
.SS pack
A pack is a git-backed context bundle (skills + knowledge + reference-only
integrations + config).  See
.B docs/design/packs.md .
.TP
.BR "pi-stack pack new" " [" PATH "]"
Adopt a pre-existing repo (or one already carrying pack.toml) as a pack, else
git-init a fresh one.  Default PATH is the personal pack root.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack pack add" " " "skill|knowledge" " " name " [" PATH "]"
Add an artifact to a pack (implicit-creates it).
.TP
.BR "pi-stack pack use" " " path-or-git-url
Set the active pack; a git URL is cloned (optional #ref pin).
.TP
.BR "pi-stack pack ls" ;
.BR "pi-stack pack show" " [" PATH "];"
.BR "pi-stack pack rm"
Inspect the active pack, show a pack, or detach the active pack (files
untouched).
.SS secret
With no subcommand,
.B secret
runs
.BR ls .
.TP
.B "pi-stack secret ls"
Report whether
.B op
is installed and has an account, and classify configured refs without printing
secret values.
.TP
.BI "pi-stack secret set " ENV_VAR " " op://vault/item/field
Upsert a ref into op-refs.env, seeding the refs-only template first if absent.
Rejects a value that is not an
.B op://
reference unless
.I ENV_VAR
is on the documented non-secret allowlist.  A raw space in the ref (a spaced
1Password field name) is URL-encoded to
.B %20 .
.TP
.BI "pi-stack secret rm " ENV_VAR
Remove a ref from op-refs.env, preserving every other line.  A no-op
(exit 0) if the key or the file is absent.
.TP
.B "pi-stack secret check"
Resolve each active
.B op://
reference using
.B op read
and print only OK or FAIL.  Missing refs state or unavailable
.B op
returns status 3; failed resolutions return status 1.
.SS config
With no subcommand,
.B config
runs
.BR show .
.TP
.B "pi-stack config show"
Print the resolved configuration path followed by decoded TOML with defaults
applied.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack config path" " [" op-refs "]"
Print the path to
.B config.toml
or, with
.BR op-refs ,
the path to
.BR op-refs.env .
.TP
.BR "pi-stack config get" " [" \-\-profile " " N "] " key
Print one resolved value with no decoration for scripts to source (list keys
print space-separated).  The value is profile-aware, resolved through the
active profile.  Unknown keys fail with a nonzero exit.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack config set" " [" \-\-profile " " N "] " key " " value
Set a scalar key or add a value to a list key, then save the configuration.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack config unset" " [" \-\-profile " " N "] " key " [" value "]"
Clear or reset a scalar key, or remove
.I value
from a list key.
.PP
Base keys are
.BR gog_account ,
.BR mcp ,
.BR services ,
.BR knowledge_bundles ,
.BR memory_watcher_model ,
.BR memory_embed_model ,
.BR ollama_bridge_model ,
.BR host.enabled ,
.BR host.autonomy ,
and
.BR host.autoserve .
.B ollama_bridge_model
is the local model the sandbox exposes to pi and the router's local option;
by default the memory watcher shares it.
.B host.enabled
gates the unsandboxed
.B "pi-stack host"
mode (default false), and
.B host.autonomy
is reserved for the host-guard strictness.
.B host.autoserve
(default true) gates the launcher's lazy auto-start of the services daemon;
the
.B PI_STACK_NO_AUTOSERVE
environment variable also disables it and wins over the config flag.
Per-profile keys are
.BR gog_account ,
.BR mcp ,
.BR knowledge_bundles ,
and
.BR kit
(the alias
.B kits
is accepted).  Profile writes create the named profile if absent.
.BR services ,
.BR memory_watcher_model ,
.BR memory_embed_model ,
.BR ollama_bridge_model ,
.BR host.enabled ,
.BR host.autonomy ,
and
.B host.autoserve
are global and reject
.BR \-\-profile .
Setting base
.B knowledge_bundles
enables the knowledge service.  A per-profile write does not; enable the global
.B knowledge
service separately.
.SS task
Run parallel tasks on one repository.  Each task is a
.B git clone --local
of the repo with its own branch and sandbox, so tasks never collide.  Commits
land in the task's clone on this host and can be pushed or fetched back.  Clones
live under
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/pi-stack/tasks/<repo>/co/<name>
outside your repo.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack task new" " " name " [" \-\-from " " REF "] [" \-\- " pi-args..." ]
Clone the repo, create the branch
.BR pi-stack/ name ,
and launch a sandbox for it.
.B \-\-from
accepts any branch, tag, or commit that exists in the repo (default
.BR HEAD ).
Everything after
.B \-\-
is passed to pi.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack task ls" " [" \-\-json "]"
List this repo's tasks with their branch, sandbox status, and git state
(dirty and unpushed markers).  Read-only.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack task path" " " name
Print the task's checkout directory and nothing else, so it composes in a
shell:
.BR "cd \"$(pi-stack task " name " path)\"" .
The convenience form
.B "pi-stack task" name "path"
is also accepted.  A missing task exits non-zero with an empty stdout.
.TP
.BR "pi-stack task rm" " " name " [" \-\-force "]"
Tear down the sandbox and the clone, guarded.  It refuses uncommitted or
unpushed work and prints the exact recovery command unless
.B \-\-force
is given.  It always snapshots the task branch to
.B refs/pi-stack/recovered/<name>
in the main repo first, and aborts before deleting the clone if
.B sbx rm
fails.  The main repo's branches are never touched.  After the sandbox is gone
and before the clone is deleted, it harvests any uncommitted doc files (see
.B "task harvest" ).
.TP
.BR "pi-stack task gc" " [" \-\-days " " N "] [" \-\-dry\-run "] [" \-\-no\-harvest "] [" \-\-artifact\-days " " N "]"
Prune clean tasks whose age exceeds
.B \-\-days
(default 7; age is the newer of the task's creation time and its checkout's git
activity).  It reuses the same guard as
.BR rm ,
so a dirty, unpushed, or still-running task is skipped and reported, never
dropped (there is no
.BR \-\-force ).
.B \-\-dry\-run
reports what would happen.  It also prunes harvested artifact snapshots older
than
.B \-\-artifact\-days
(default 30).
.TP
.BR "pi-stack task harvest" " " name " [" \-\-to " " DIR "]"
Copy the task's uncommitted doc files (untracked, ignored, or modified-vs-HEAD)
to
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/pi-stack/artifacts/<repo>/<name>/<timestamp>/
with a
.B manifest.json
of provenance.  Idempotent and safe to run any time.  With
.B \-\-to
it copies to a caller-chosen dir instead (refused if that dir is the checkout
itself).  Harvested docs live under
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
so they survive a
.BR "pi-stack state reset" .
.SS state
The
.B state
noun groups the stack's on-disk lifecycle commands.  The flat spellings
.BR backup ,
.BR restore ,
.BR reset ,
and
.BR uninstall
keep working as aliases.
.TP
.B "pi-stack state backup"
Alias for
.BR backup .
.TP
.B "pi-stack state restore"
Alias for
.BR restore .
.TP
.B "pi-stack state reset"
Alias for
.BR reset .
.TP
.B "pi-stack state uninstall"
Alias for
.BR uninstall .
.SS reset
.TP
.BR "pi-stack reset" " [" \-\-keep-memory "] [" \-\-sbx "] [" \-\-force "] [" \-\-yes "|" \-y "]"
Stop host services and move configuration and data aside to timestamped
.B .bak-*
siblings.  Nothing is hard-deleted.
.B \-\-keep-memory
preserves captured memory while resetting other state.
.B \-\-sbx
also attempts, on a best-effort basis, to remove all
.B pi-stack-*
sandboxes and unregister configured MCP servers.  Failures in those sbx actions
are reported but do not fail the reset.
.B \-\-force
allows data movement if the service still appears to be running.
.BR \-\-yes
or
.B \-y
suppresses confirmation and is required on a non-interactive terminal.
.SS uninstall
.TP
.BR "pi-stack uninstall" " [" \-\-keep-memory "] [" \-\-purge-data "] [" \-\-force "] [" \-\-yes "|" \-y "]"
Perform the reset action, then remove the installed
.B pi-stack
and
.B pi-stack-host
entries from
.B ~/.local/bin
only when they are symlinks to pi-stack binaries.  If reset fails, leave the
symlinks installed.  Harvested task artifacts under
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/pi-stack/artifacts
are user work product and are KEPT by default (their path and size are printed);
pass
.B \-\-purge-data
to move them aside too.
.SS "version"
.TP
.B "pi-stack version"
Print the stamped launcher version.  The aliases
.B \-v
and
.B \-\-version
are accepted.
.SS help
.TP
.BR "pi-stack help" " [" verb "]"
Print general help or the authoritative usage text for an implemented verb.
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
are top-level aliases.
.SS man
.TP
.B "pi-stack man"
Render this manual page.  The same page is also produced by
.B pi-stack help --man
(the folded invocation) and by the global
.B \-\-man
alias.  The page is embedded in the binary, so no
.B MANPATH
entry is required.  The rendered page is produced through the first available
of
.BR mandoc (1),
.BR man (1),
or
.BR groff (1);
if none is present the raw roff source is written out.  When standard output is
not a terminal, the page is written directly without a pager, so
.B "pi-stack man"
can be piped or redirected.  The global alias
.B \-\-man
is accepted anywhere on the command line.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " NAME"
Select a named profile.  The option may appear before or after the command, but
must appear before a
.B \-\-
argument terminator.  The
.BI \-\-profile= NAME
form is also accepted.  An empty profile name is an error.  Commands that resolve a profile reject an
unknown name.  A profile-scoped
.B "config set"
or
.B "config unset"
may instead create the named profile.  Backup, restore, global knowledge
init/use, config show/path, setup, serve, version, and lifecycle file movement
are not profile-scoped.  For profile-aware commands, the option selects the
context used by run, status, doctor, memory RPCs, knowledge query/list/sync,
MCP registration, and profile configuration writes.
.SH PROFILES
A profile is a named override set layered on the base configuration.  It may
override
.BR gog_account ,
.BR mcp ,
.BR knowledge_bundles ,
and
.BR kits.stack .
A present list replaces the base list; an absent list inherits it.  The implicit
.B default
profile is the base configuration.
.PP
Selection precedence is:
.B \-\-profile
first,
.B PI_STACK_PROFILE
second,
.B active_profile
from configuration third, and
.B default
last.  The host supervisor indexes the union of knowledge bundles from all
profiles; queries are scoped to the selected profile.
.SH FILES
.SS "Configuration tree"
.TP
.B ~/.config/pi-stack/config.toml
Machine-managed TOML configuration.  The directory and file may move under
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
or
.BR PI_STACK_CONFIG .
Schema fields are
.BR version_pin ,
.BR services ,
.BR mcp ,
.BR active_profile ,
.BR memory_watcher_model ,
.BR memory_embed_model ,
.BR gog_account ,
.BR knowledge_bundles ,
.BR kits.stack ,
.BR skills.paths ,
.BR profiles.* ,
and
.BR plugins.* .
.TP
.B ~/.config/pi-stack/op-refs.env
Refs-only environment file for host MCP credentials.  Secret entries are
.B op://vault/item/field
references resolved by
.BR op (1).
.TP
.B ~/.config/pi-stack/broker-token
Mode-0600 bearer shared by launcher and host broker components.
.TP
.B ~/.config/pi-stack/serve.pid
PID of the running
.B pi-stack-host serve
supervisor.
.TP
.B ~/.config/pi-stack/knowledge/
Default global OKF bundle created by
.BR "knowledge init" .
.TP
.B ~/.config/pi-stack/knowledge-cache/
Cached clones for knowledge bundle Git URLs.
.SS "Data tree"
.TP
.B ~/.local/share/pi-stack/memory/memory.db
Default memory SQLite database.
.TP
.B ~/.local/share/pi-stack/knowledge/knowledge.db
Default knowledge SQLite index.
.TP
.B ~/.local/share/pi-stack/backups/pi-stack-backup-*.tar.gz
Default full backup archives (memory, config, and op-refs).
.SS "Workspace and installation files"
.TP
.B .pi-stack/knowledge
Committed per-project knowledge bundle pointer.
.TP
.B .pi-stack/knowledge.scope
Launcher-generated canonical bundle scope for the current run.
.TP
.B .pi-stack/profile
Launcher-generated active memory profile for the current run.
.TP
.B ~/.local/bin/pi-stack
Installed launcher symlink removed by
.BR uninstall .
.TP
.B ~/.local/bin/pi-stack-host
Installed host-service symlink removed by
.BR uninstall .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PI_STACK_CONFIG
Use this exact path for
.BR config.toml .
Its parent also contains
.BR op-refs.env ,
.BR broker-token ,
and
.BR serve.pid .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Use
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pi-stack
as the configuration directory when
.B PI_STACK_CONFIG
is unset.
.TP
.B PI_STACK_PROFILE
Select a profile when
.B \-\-profile
is absent.
.TP
.B PI_STACK_DEV_ROOT
Directory of a pi-stack checkout used by
.BR "run --dev" .
It must contain
.BR pi-kit/spec.yaml .
.TP
.B PI_STACK_DEBUG
When non-empty, print the composed
.B sbx
command before running it.
.TP
.B PI_STACK_OP_REFS
Override the refs file included by
.BR backup .
This does not change the canonical refs path used by
.BR "secret" .
.TP
.B MEMORY_DB
Override the memory database path, normally
.BR ~/.local/share/pi-stack/memory/memory.db .
Reset, backup, and restore honor it.
.TP
.B KNOWLEDGE_DB
Override the knowledge index path, normally
.BR ~/.local/share/pi-stack/knowledge/knowledge.db .
Reset honors it.
.TP
.B MEMORY_PORT
Override memory port 11435 for the host service, launcher RPC client, status,
reset guard, restore service probe, and serve control.
.TP
.B KNOWLEDGE_PORT
Override knowledge port 11436 for the host service, launcher RPC client, and
serve control.
.TP
.B MEMORY_WATCHER_MODEL
Override the Ollama fact-capture model.  The configuration default is
.BR gemma3:4b
(small, so the resident watcher fits a 16GB machine).
.TP
.B MEMORY_EMBED_MODEL
Override the Ollama embedding model used by memory and knowledge.  The
configuration default is
.BR nomic-embed-text .
.TP
.B KNOWLEDGE_BUNDLES
Comma- or platform path-list-separated bundle paths used by the host supervisor
only when configured knowledge bundles, including profile bundles, are empty.
.TP
.B SBX_MCP_URL
Enable sbx MCP operations.  MCP registration requires it; run-time MCP
attachment is omitted when it is unset.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, displayed status, an accepted abort, or help.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration, filesystem, Git, subprocess, RPC-response, or other operational
failure.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command, option, argument, or other usage error.
.TP
.B 3
A required host service or secret-check dependency is unreachable.  Memory and
knowledge RPC commands use this status for a down daemon.
.PP
Commands that execute
.BR sbx ,
.BR pi-stack-host ,
or another child process may propagate that process's exit status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Apply host-side config non-interactively (CI); conversational onboarding is
in-session via
.BR run :
.PP
.nf
.RS
pi-stack onboard --account you@example.com --yes
.RE
.fi
.PP
Launch the current directory, then launch a project under the work profile:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pi-stack run
pi-stack --profile work run ~/src/project
.RE
.fi
.PP
Recall and record memory:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pi-stack memory recall "release process" --limit 5
pi-stack memory remember "Release tags use the v prefix"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Push knowledge changes to a review branch:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pi-stack knowledge sync -m "knowledge: update release process"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Set a 1Password reference and stop host services:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pi-stack secret set SLACK_TOKEN op://Private/Slack/credential
pi-stack serve stop
.RE
.fi
.PP
Reset state while preserving captured memory:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pi-stack reset --keep-memory
.RE
.fi
.PP
Back up and restore the full stack (memory, config, op-refs):
.PP
.nf
.RS
pi-stack backup --out ~/backups/pi-stack.tar.gz
pi-stack serve stop
pi-stack restore ~/backups/pi-stack.tar.gz --force
.RE
.fi
.PP
Run two isolated tasks on one repo in parallel, then tear one down:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pi-stack task new fix-login
pi-stack task new refactor-db --from origin/main
pi-stack task ls
pi-stack task rm fix-login
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pi (1),
.BR sbx (1),
.BR op (1)
